WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.510
- [Instructor] What we're
going to do in this video

00:00:01.510 --> 00:00:04.840
is do some practice examples
of exercises on Khan Academy

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:07.070
that deal with reflections of functions.

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:09.870
So this first one says this
is the graph of function f.

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:10.770
Fair enough.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:14.330
Function g is defined as g of
x is equal to f of negative x.

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:15.500
Also fair enough.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:16.770
What is the graph of g?

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.450
And on Khan Academy, it's multiple choice,

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.210
but I thought for the sake of this video,

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:22.630
it'd be fun to think about
what g would look like

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:24.810
without having any choices,
just sketching it out.

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:26.420
So pause this video and
try to think about it,

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:27.920
at least in your head.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:29.850
All right, now let's work
through this together.

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:32.340
So we've already gone
over that g of x is equal

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.570
to f of negative x.

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:38.780
So whatever the value of
f is at a certain value,

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.750
we would expect g to take on that value

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:43.530
at the negative of that.

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:48.000
So for example, we can see
that f of four is equal to two,

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.800
so we would expect g of
negative four to be equal to two

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.160
because, once again, g of negative four,

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:56.040
we could write it over here.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:59.250
G of negative four is going to be equal

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:02.810
to f of the negative of negative four,

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:05.650
which is equal to f of four.

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:07.930
And so we could keep going with that.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:11.260
What would g of negative two be?

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:12.950
Well, that would be the
same thing as f of two,

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:15.690
which is zero, so it
would be right over there.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.580
What would g of zero be?

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:20.300
Well, that would be the
same thing as f of zero

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:21.590
'cause a negative zero is zero.

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:24.320
And f of zero is right over there.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:25.590
Looks like negative two.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:28.240
And so you can already
see where this is going.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.810
And we've already talked
about it in previous videos

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.810
that if you replace your
x with a negative x,

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:37.210
you're essentially
reflecting over the y-axis.

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:41.360
So g is going to look something like this.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:42.970
It is going to look something like this.

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:45.610
Once again, g of negative
six would be the same thing

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:47.060
as f of six.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.760
And so that would be the graph of g.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:50.810
And if you're doing this on Khan Academy,

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:52.470
you'd pick the choice
that looks like this,

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:55.980
that would give a
reflection over the y-axis.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:57.973
Let's do another example.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:02.800
So here once again, this is
the graph of the function f.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:05.600
And then they say what is the graph of g?

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.330
And so pause this video and at least try

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:10.830
to sketch it out in your
mind what g should look like.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.120
All right, so in this situation,

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.840
they didn't replace the x
with negative x in f of x.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:20.300
Instead, g of x is equal to
the negative of all of f of x.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.280
In fact, we could
rewrite g of x like this.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:24.700
We could say that g of
x is equal to, notice,

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:25.750
all of this right over here,

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:28.050
that was our definition of f of x.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:31.610
So g of x is equal to
the negative of f of x.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:33.460
So instead of it being f of negative x,

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:35.820
it's equal to the negative of f of x.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:38.150
So one way to think about it is

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:41.060
we can see that f of zero is two,

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:43.250
but g of zero is going to
be the negative of that.

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:46.150
So it's going to be equal to negative two.

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:48.680
And so you could keep going with that.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:52.970
You could see that whatever
f of a certain value is,

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:55.740
g of that value would
be the negative of that.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:57.430
So it would be down here.

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:01.120
And so g of x would be
a reflection of f of x

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.860
about the x-axis.

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:05.710
So g of x is going to look

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:07.477
something like that,

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:15.140
a reflection about the x-axis.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:16.530
Once again, on Khan Academy,
you'd pick the choice

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:18.620
that would actually look like that.

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:19.453
Let's do another example.

00:03:19.453 --> 00:03:22.107
His is strangely fun.

00:03:22.107 --> 00:03:25.760
(laughs) All right, so here
we're told functions f,

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:27.480
so that's in solid in this blue color,

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:31.190
and g dashed, so that's right
over there, are graphed.

00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:34.970
What is the equation of g in terms of f?

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:37.760
So pause this video and
try to think about it.

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:42.060
So the key is to realize
how do we transform f of x,

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:43.290
actually, they've labeled it over here,

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:46.810
this is f of x right over
here, in order to get g?

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:50.120
So f of negative x would be a reflection

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:52.600
of f about the y-axis.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.720
And so it would intersect there.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.300
It would have this
straight portion like this.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:01.730
And I'm just experimenting right now.

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:03.370
It'd have the straight portion like this.

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:05.930
And then it would go up.

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:07.183
And let's see.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:09.570
If f of negative x.

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:11.460
So when you input six into it,

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.770
that would be f of
negative six, which is six.

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:15.290
So it would go up there.

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:20.290
So f of negative x would
look something like this.

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:23.110
Something like that.

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:25.750
So the purple is f of negative x.

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:28.910
Now, that doesn't quite get us to g,

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:30.030
but it gets us a little bit closer

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:31.670
'cause it looks like if I
were to take the reflection

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:36.060
of f of negative x, f of
negative x about the x-axis,

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:38.510
it looks like I'm going to get to g.

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:40.980
And so how do you reflect
something about the x-axis?

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:43.130
Well, we saw it in the example just now.

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:45.590
You multiply the entire
function by a negative.

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:47.390
So we could say that g is equal

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:51.200
to the negative of f of negative x.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:52.530
It's equal to the negative of this.

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:54.270
So we're doing both reflections.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:55.820
We're flipping over the y-axis,

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:59.240
and we're flipping over
the x-axis to get to g.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:01.183
Let's do one more example.

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:05.910
So once again, they've
graphed f, they've graphed g,

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.880
and they've said f is defined
as this right over here.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:10.780
What is the equation of g?

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:12.930
So they're not just
asking it in terms of f.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:14.940
They just wanna know what
is the equation of g?

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:17.460
Pause this video and
try to think about it.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:19.540
Well, you can see pretty clearly

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:23.260
that this is a reflection
across the y-axis.

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:26.050
And a reflection across the y-axis,

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:28.090
you can see pretty clearly,

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:33.090
that g of x is equal to f of negative x.

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:34.590
F of negative x.

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:36.070
How do we know that?

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:40.830
Well, for whenever we take f
of x and we get that value,

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:43.570
g at the negative of that value

00:05:43.570 --> 00:05:46.500
takes on the same function
value, I guess I could say.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:47.860
Or another way to think about it is

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:49.380
we could just pick this
point, negative eight.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:52.780
F of negative eight is
equal to a little over four,

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:57.210
but g of eight is equal
to a little over four,

00:05:57.210 --> 00:05:59.180
is equal to that same value.

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:01.140
And so what is the equation of g?

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:03.340
Well, we just have to rewrite this

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:05.800
so that we can write
it out as an equation.

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:09.080
And so we could write
out g of x is equal to.

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:10.930
If I were to replace all of the x's here

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:12.340
with a negative x, what would I get?

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:17.090
I would get four times the
square root of two minus.

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:19.720
Instead of an x, I will have a negative x.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:23.030
And then the minus eight
is outside of the radical.

00:06:23.030 --> 00:06:25.240
And so we would have g of x is equal

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:30.240
to four times the square root
of two plus x minus eight.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:31.433
And we're done.

